            <h2>
    
    <!-- THEME ICON MACRO watch -->
    <svg viewBox="0 0 232.18 168.39" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" ><path d="m0 146v16.58a5.77 5.77 0 0 0 5.78 5.78h220.58a5.78 5.78 0 0 0 5.78-5.78v-16.58z" fill="#00a6e0"/><path d="m232.18 146.21v-140.43a5.78 5.78 0 0 0 -5.78-5.78h-220.59a5.78 5.78 0 0 0 -5.81 5.78v140.43z" fill="#00a6e0"/><path d="m9.54 9.31h213.13v127.59h-213.13z" fill="#4a4f55"/><ellipse cx="116.11" cy="71.74" fill="#ddf2fb" rx="40.37" ry="40.15"/><path d="m116.11 112.57a40.83 40.83 0 1 1 41.05-40.83 41 41 0 0 1 -41.05 40.83zm0-80.3a39.47 39.47 0 1 0 39.68 39.47 39.62 39.62 0 0 0 -39.68-39.47z" fill="#32b6eb"/><path d="m101.03 98.26v-50.32l43.81 25.16z" fill="#00a6e0"/></svg>
Watch</h2>
              <p>Let’s now listen to a discussion on the importance of confidentiality in relation to supporting individuals with autism.</p>
<h3>Interview with professionals / key workers</h3>
<p>Please see the following interview video of professionals' responses to the following question:</p>
<p>Why do you think it is important to maintain confidentiality when supporting individuals with autism?</p>

              <!-- sections.njk VIDEO! -->
              
              
              
      <div class="l-video-container">
        <video poster="~/images/svg/poster.svg" controls preload="metadata" aria-labelledby="watch" width="1000"
          data-base="ua_u2_s2_p4_interview_maintain_confidentiality" 
          data-ext=".mp4" 
          data-multi="false"
          data-trans="ua_u2_s2_p4_interview_maintain_confidentiality undefined">
          <source src="https://kpcontent.blob.core.windows.net/$web/resources/v2l/720/ua_u2_s2_p4_interview_maintain_confidentiality.mp4" type="video/mp4">
          <track default label="English" kind="captions" srclang="en" src="~/videos/captions/vtt/ua_u2_s2_p4_interview_maintain_confidentiality.vtt">
          <p>Sorry, your browser doesn't support embedded videos</p>
        </video>
      </div>
      
          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p>Phil: Well, I think we've confidentiality in general with any sort of data protection for any human being is important. And the one thing, the main thing, you know, people with autism are still people whether people prefer &quot;people with autism&quot;, because you put the people first or &quot;autistic people&quot;.</p>
<p>Whatever your preference of wording is you're still people, so you should really... the confidentiality should be respected as you would expect any other person, really.</p>
<p>Kerry: I think it's important to maintain confidentiality with anybody that you work with, you know, cause I was a mainstream teacher and now I work in a special needs school, it's important to have that confidentiality with every people.</p>
<p>Zoe: It's very important to maintain confidentiality when working with children with autism. It's important that not sharing information that they may not want to be shared. And so when sharing information, it's important to get the consent from the child or young person to actually share.</p>
<p>Ellen: Because you need that level of trust. And if I broke that trust, I mean, it's taken me about a year, cause I've only been here about a year, to build those relationships with the young people. So if they kind of found out that I had said something about them, about their personal lives or whatever, they probably wouldn't trust me.</p>
<p>Zoe T: Whatever they want to remain confidential, I think, other than if it was a safeguarding issue, if the, you know, whatever it is that needed to be confidential from their perspective, but if it was going to be a safeguarding issue, then obviously you couldn't keep that confidential.</p>
<p>Ellen: We can obviously go to our safeguarding leads if we're worried about that young person to kind of get them the best help, but you wouldn't see us kind of going outside of here and being like, oh, you know, &quot;child A has done blah, blah, blah&quot;.</p>
<p>Phil: The chances are there's going to be as well as a diagnosis of autism, chances are, there's going to be lots of other additional needs around that as we often find with our pupils - might be mental health issues, of a Fitbit or physical health issues. Might be family trauma and things like that.</p>
<p>Zoe T: Well, I think that has to be led by the person with autism themselves or the family, if the person with autism can't advocate for themselves.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
