    <svg viewBox="0 0 232.18 168.39" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" ><path d="m0 146v16.58a5.77 5.77 0 0 0 5.78 5.78h220.58a5.78 5.78 0 0 0 5.78-5.78v-16.58z" fill="#00a6e0"/><path d="m232.18 146.21v-140.43a5.78 5.78 0 0 0 -5.78-5.78h-220.59a5.78 5.78 0 0 0 -5.81 5.78v140.43z" fill="#00a6e0"/><path d="m9.54 9.31h213.13v127.59h-213.13z" fill="#4a4f55"/><ellipse cx="116.11" cy="71.74" fill="#ddf2fb" rx="40.37" ry="40.15"/><path d="m116.11 112.57a40.83 40.83 0 1 1 41.05-40.83 41 41 0 0 1 -41.05 40.83zm0-80.3a39.47 39.47 0 1 0 39.68 39.47 39.62 39.62 0 0 0 -39.68-39.47z" fill="#32b6eb"/><path d="m101.03 98.26v-50.32l43.81 25.16z" fill="#00a6e0"/></svg>
Watch</h2>
              <h3>Interview with professionals / key workers</h3>
<p>Please see the following interview video of professionals' responses to the following question:</p>
<p>Tell us about the sort of information that should be kept confidential and why?</p>

              <!-- sections.njk VIDEO! -->
              
              
              
      <div class="l-video-container">
        <video poster="~/images/svg/poster.svg" controls preload="metadata" aria-labelledby="watch" width="1000"
          data-base="ua_u2_s2_p4_interview_confidential_types" 
          data-ext=".mp4" 
          data-multi="false"
          data-trans="ua_u2_s2_p4_interview_confidential_types undefined">
          <source src="https://kpcontent.blob.core.windows.net/$web/resources/v2l/720/ua_u2_s2_p4_interview_confidential_types.mp4" type="video/mp4">
          <track default label="English" kind="captions" srclang="en" src="~/videos/captions/vtt/ua_u2_s2_p4_interview_confidential_types.vtt">
          <p>Sorry, your browser doesn't support embedded videos</p>
        </video>
      </div>
      
          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p>Zoe: The sort of information that should be kept confidential is anything related to the child and young person, anything that could be perceived as personal, definitely, but anything related to their education.</p>
<p>Kerry: Would be those that, from my experience, it's things like around toileting needs, medications, yeah. any further diagnoses that the young people might have, you know, are only shared with those people that it is relevant to, to give them the better support.</p>
<p>Ellen: Their needs as well, because every student has their own plan and it tells us in there what their reading ages are what their kind of development is, mental, kind of development. So all of that, we would keep confidential. We couldn't share that outside of here.</p>
<p>Zoe: Even if I'm having a conversation with a child about their experience in the classroom. If I'm planning to pass that information on, I'll always talk to the child and check they're happy with me passing it on.</p>
<p>Phil: If it's me that identified that a pupil needs to see, for instance, a counselor or play therapist, they may benefit from that, for instance, due to a childhood trauma that they need to process and play out. I mean, I don't pretend to understand the process entirely, but what I do know is that that session is between them and the play therapist or the counselor for them to work out and it's led by them and whilst people, other members of staff supporting those people, know that they may be attending these sessions, the content is important too.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
